Z||000004||Bob Englert||03-21-2001||12:07 PM||||BToth:<P>Oil pressure dropping in the red at idle is a sign of an engine that will probably need major attention in the not to distant future.  Sounds like the lower end bearings have worn and/or the oil pump may be worn.  <P>Both of my 87 GT's have low oil pressure at hot idle.  <P>Bet bet to check it is to install a mechanical oil pressure gauge and see the actual pressure.  Other possible causes could be a defective or failing oil pressure sending unit, and the oil filter bypass valve should be checked and probably be replaced (it is real cheap).  If not these , then you probably have a rebuild somewhere in your future.  That's what I am expecting in the future for both of mine.<P>Great excuse to put that 3.4 you have always wanted!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||12:15 PM||||mine holds about 50 psi at idle and jumps to about 70 when driving, this is after a rebuild about 12000 miles ago. However even the old motor with somewhere in the neighborhood of 100K+ still maintained about 40 at idle and 60 or so at highway speed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||03-21-2001||04:17 PM||||im about at 50 hot idle, 75 running. Ive had cars that had only about 5 pnds at hot idle that ran for years like that. My lincoln red lite flashed at idle for last 50K miles, still running fine far as I know. More important is pressure running, needs at least 35-40 minimum.||||reg||1
